## Unexpected Dominance in Recalls

The recall landscape has been notably active this year, with a total of 781 recalls to date, including 217 classified as Class I--the most serious type. Not surprisingly, allergens continue to dominate the causes, accounting for 311 of the recalls.

### Spicing Up the Recall Game

Among the 11 recent recalls reported on July 15, 2026, none stood out more than the actions involving Adams Extract & Spice LLC. With four separate entries, all for undeclared sesame allergens in various spice blends, Adams Extract has positioned itself less as a provider of culinary enhancement and more as a cautionary tale in allergen management. Their biggest blunder? The "Smoked Sausage Spice" and "Coopers BBQ 60 Gal Bean Mix Box." For a company dealing in precision formulations like spice blends, this lack of allergen transparency is especially inexcusable.

### Contextual Analysis

Adams isn't alone in its lackluster performance. The data reveals a broader trend of repeat offenders: 95 brands have had multiple recalls this year alone, and many, like Spring & Mulberry Inc., maintain failing grades in safety compliance.

### Other Notable Mentions

Brands such as "Fresh & Ready Foods LLC" also encountered significant issues recently, with raw chicken patties mislabeled as ready-to-eat, posing severe salmonella risks.

### Final Take

While the USDA and FDA play their parts in surveillance and enforcement, it is clear that both manufacturers and consumers must remain vigilant. Brands need to adopt proactive measures, such as enhanced ingredient testing and improved labeling protocols, while consumers should remain cautious, especially with products from companies with known compliance failures.

This week's recalls remind us once again: the devil is in the details, especially when food safety is concerned.
